# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## Repository purpose

<<TOPIC_BLURB>>

It is a *reference / discussion harness*, not the implementation repo. It bundles the literature library, project notes, and the Zulip bridge to the `<<ZULIP_STREAM>>` stream on `zulip.hkust-gz.edu.cn`.

Git remote: `<<GITHUB_REMOTE>>` (private).

## First-time setup (per machine)

**Easiest: just say "onboard me" or "/onboard"** — the `onboard` skill in `.claude/skills/onboard/` walks through the steps interactively, detecting what you already have and only prompting for what's missing.

Manual checklist if you'd rather DIY:

1. **Claude Code installed** — `.claude/skills/{onboard,download-ref,zulip-reply}/` auto-load when you open this directory with `claude`. No manual registration.
2. **`zlp` CLI** — `pip install zlp-cli`. Used by every `make zulip-*` target.
3. **A hkust-gz Zulip API key** — log in at <https://zulip.hkust-gz.edu.cn>, then *Personal settings → Account & privacy → API key → Show/change your API key → Download zuliprc*. Save the file to:
   ```
   ~/zulip-workspaces/hkust-gz/zuliprc
   ```
   `chmod 600` it (it's an API key). That path is the default `ZULIP_WORKSPACE`. To use a different location, set `export ZULIP_WORKSPACE=/your/path` in your shell rc.
4. **`pymupdf4llm`** (only needed when *adding new references* via the `download-ref` skill) — see the skill's "Preflight" section. Skip this until you actually need it.

Verify with `make zulip-whoami` — should print your account.

## Common commands

All `make` targets are Zulip-bridge wrappers (this repo has no build step yet — see "What is *not* in this repo" below). Run from the repo root.

```sh
# Verify auth (run once after cloning on a new machine)
make zulip-whoami                       # → prints server URL + account

# Inspect the stream
make zulip-topics                       # list topics
make zulip-messages TOPIC=<topic-name> LIMIT=5
make zulip-messages TOPIC=<topic-name> LIMIT=20 FORMAT=md

# Mirror messages into .zulip/ (gitignored)
make zulip-pull                         # incremental, all topics
make zulip-pull TOPIC=<topic-name>      # narrow to one topic
make zulip-pull IMPORT_HISTORY=1        # first-time backfill / reset cursor

# Post a message (creates the topic if it doesn't exist — verify spelling first)
make zulip-send TOPIC=<topic-name> MSG="hello"
make zulip-send TOPIC=<topic-name> MSG_FILE=/tmp/msg.md
```

Adding references to `.knowledge/` is done via the `download-ref` skill, not `make` — see "Adding a new reference" below.

## Knowledge base (`.knowledge/`) — check first

When answering any technical question on this harness's topics, **search `.knowledge/` before anything else**.

- `.knowledge/INDEX.md` — table of contents grouped by source (arXiv / DOI), with title, authors, year, venue, full-text availability. Auto-regenerated by the `download-ref` skill.
- One markdown file per paper at `.knowledge/<id>_<slug>.md` with YAML frontmatter (`arxiv_id` / `doi`, `title`, `authors`, `full_text: yes|no`).
- `.knowledge/.raw/` and `.knowledge/.figures/` — original PDFs and extracted images. Gitignored. Exclude from searches.

Search recipe (run from repo root):

```
rg --hidden -g '!.raw' -g '!.figures' "term" .knowledge/
```

If a relevant paper isn't in `.knowledge/`, use the `download-ref` skill rather than answering from generic prior knowledge.

## Adding a new reference (`download-ref` skill)

`download-ref` is the entry point for any "add this arXiv/DOI to the KB" request. It is documented in `.claude/skills/download-ref/SKILL.md` and calls helpers under `.claude/skills/download-ref/helpers/` (in-tree, no external dependency):

1. `fetch_metadata.py --kb .knowledge --manifest <json> --download-arxiv-pdfs`
2. `render.py --kb .knowledge` (uses `pymupdf4llm` if available — preserves figures; falls back to `markitdown` then `pdftotext`)
3. `index.py --kb .knowledge --title "<<TOPIC>> — references" --source-note "Reading list and full-text harness for the <<TOPIC>> project. arXiv preprints, DOIs (with arXiv-preprint fallbacks where the publisher gates the PDF)."`

Invariants the skill assumes:
- The renderer needs `pymupdf4llm` available to whichever Python `python3` resolves to. Anaconda's pymupdf4llm sometimes doesn't satisfy it — `which python3` first.
- `--source-note` and `--title` strings must stay stable across runs or `INDEX.md` will churn. Copy them verbatim from the existing `INDEX.md` header on every re-run.

When `download-ref` finishes, **do not** auto-append to `ref.bib` unless this repo has a LaTeX draft (`main.tex`) — the append step in the skill is dormant until then.

## Zulip channel (`.zulip/`) — discussion archive

Co-author chat lives in the **`<<ZULIP_STREAM>>`** stream on `zulip.hkust-gz.edu.cn`. The `Makefile` at the repo root wraps the [`zlp`](https://pypi.org/project/zlp-cli/) CLI to mirror that stream into `.zulip/` (gitignored).

The Makefile pins the workspace + stream and exports the env vars `zlp` needs (`ZULIP_CONFIG_FILE`, `ZLP_ARCHIVE_ROOT`, `ZLP_RUN_ROOT`) from `~/zulip-workspaces/hkust-gz/`. If `zlp` isn't installed: `pip install zlp-cli`. Commands are listed in the "Common commands" section above.

Caveats / gotchas:
- Stream-wide pulls land in a hash-suffixed folder like `_all-<hash>/` (a `zlp` slugify quirk for the placeholder topic name). Topic-narrowed pulls produce the cleaner `<topic-slug>/` folder.
- `make zulip-pull` mirrors the **user's own outgoing messages** as `.md` files too. Filter by `make zulip-whoami` display name when summarizing co-author traffic.
- `make zulip-send` silently creates a new topic if `TOPIC=` doesn't match an existing one (case- and whitespace-sensitive). Verify with `make zulip-topics | grep -F "<topic>"` before sending.
- Never add an AI-attribution signature to outgoing Zulip messages. The account name is the only attribution.

## Doing common tasks

**"What's the latest on <subtopic>?"** → `rg "<keyword>" .knowledge/` first; then read the matched `.md` files.

**"Add this arXiv ref"** → Use the `download-ref` skill. Manifest accepts both `arxiv` and `doi` lists; the skill is idempotent.

**"Post links to Zulip"** → Draft to `/tmp/<file>.md`, then `make zulip-send TOPIC="<topic>" MSG_FILE=/tmp/<file>.md`. Match the existing message style in the target topic (concise, numbered, `[Title](URL)`); check with `make zulip-messages TOPIC="<topic>" LIMIT=5` first.

**"Check Zulip" / "what did X say" / "draft a reply"** → invoke the **`zulip-reply` skill** (in `.claude/skills/zulip-reply/`). It pulls new messages, grounds each in `.knowledge/`, walks them one-by-one with quoted-body context, and asks before sending.

## What is *not* in this repo

- No LaTeX documents yet (no `main.tex`, `pretex.tex`, `ref.bib`). The LaTeX entries in `.gitignore` are inherited from the harness template — keep them; they'll be live once a draft is added. The `download-ref` skill's "append to ref.bib" step is dormant until then.
- No code under version control beyond skills + Makefile. Implementation goes in linked external repos (list them in this section once you have them).

